WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.674
Every model has practical token budgets.

00:00:02.674 --> 00:00:10.829
Input tokens include instructions, source material, conversation history, examples, retrieved documents, and tool outputs.

00:00:10.829 --> 00:00:14.105
Output tokens are the answer the model generates.

00:00:14.105 --> 00:00:16.578
A request has to leave room for both.

00:00:16.578 --> 00:00:26.471
If the input is too large, the system may reject the request, truncate material, or leave too little space for the response the user actually needs.

00:00:26.471 --> 00:00:29.078
Hosted models often tie tokens to cost.

00:00:29.078 --> 00:00:37.969
Sending a large body of text may cost more than sending a focused prompt, and asking for a long response increases the total further.

00:00:37.969 --> 00:00:44.119
Local models have different economics, but they still pay in RAM, VRAM, and processing time.

00:00:44.119 --> 00:00:51.338
Large contexts can increase prompt processing time and memory pressure even when there is no per-token bill.

00:00:51.338 --> 00:00:55.884
Latency matters because long context changes the feel of a workflow.

00:00:55.884 --> 00:01:01.031
A model may take longer to read the prompt before generating the first token.

00:01:01.031 --> 00:01:05.443
Generation time may also increase if the requested output is long.

00:01:05.443 --> 00:01:13.330
For local models, the selected context length can affect how much memory is reserved and how stable the runtime feels.

00:01:13.330 --> 00:01:17.007
Maximum context is not always the best operating point.

00:01:17.007 --> 00:01:21.954
Good context management balances performance, quality, and workload needs.

00:01:21.954 --> 00:01:26.767
If the task requires a full source set, the extra cost may be justified.

00:01:26.767 --> 00:01:31.646
If the task needs only a focused answer, a smaller context may be better.

00:01:31.646 --> 00:01:37.061
Engineers should treat context length as a tuning parameter, not a trophy number.

00:01:37.061 --> 00:01:44.548
The right amount of context is the amount that improves the outcome without unnecessary cost, latency, or noise.
